{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c66d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06e98-2a6f-7230-81f2-5355ec816701"},"sequence":401,"recorded_at":1780531400000797,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"e5781de4-97b1-4e5e-9466-20c1d6e7652b","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"e5781de4-97b1-4e5e-9466-20c1d6e7652b","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"e5781de4-97b1-4e5e-9466-20c1d6e7652b"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"b0fa7d72-adde-4e15-8fb7-f09a2c86d359"},"task_id":"b0fa7d72-adde-4e15-8fb7-f09a2c86d359","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"b0fa7d72-adde-4e15-8fb7-f09a2c86d359","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-14-1\",\n  \"command\": \"R=/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd; grep -l -i 'logbook' $R/* 2>/dev/null; echo ===; grep -n -i -A30 'logbook' \\\"$R/TASK-260830-2890sd_review-verdict.md\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -80\",\n  \"description\": \"Find logbook entries in verdicts\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_change-request_rev1-validation.log\\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_change-request_rev2-validation.log\\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_change-request_rev3-validation.log\\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_review-verdict-rev2.md\\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_review-verdict.md\\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--muse-_RUN-260904-4b94f5.log\\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--muse-_RUN-260904-b8a3ca.log\\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--muse-_RUN-260904-e8955d.log\\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_spawn-log_-reviewer--reviewer--claude-_RUN-260904-067bc2.log\\n===\\n23:`doc.go`), seven test files, a README section, and a LOGBOOK entry. The\\n24-verdict below is about that delta, not about the empty patch.\\n25-\\n26-## What holds\\n27-\\n28-The production behaviour is correct as written and matches §7.1 clause by\\n29-clause. `Discover`/`Trust`/`Verify` are the production entry points, driven\\n30-directly by tests through both the fake seam and the real `OSSystem` on a\\n31-temporary tree.\\n32-\\n33-27 of 32 mutants were caught, including narrowing mutants — not only\\n34-deletions — across the duplicate refusal, the discovery source order and\\n35-bytewise sort, the PATH gate, the plugin_dirs absolute-path gate, the\\n36-regular-file and owner gates, symlink resolution, malformed-name refusal,\\n37-and every read-failure-as-absence path in both `Discover` and `Verify`. The\\n38-duplicate refusal is proven behaviourally on the real filesystem with a live\\n39-script fixture whose sentinel stays absent. The refusal-inventory gate is\\n40-real: it independently caught a newly added unexercised refusal site and a\\n41-stray `Error` composite literal when I attacked it. Reported facts I\\n42-re-derived — the single-hash digest path, fail-closed `digestBytes`, the\\n43-hash-pinned ownership registry — all check out. Full details and the mutant\\n44-table are in `TASK-260830-2890sd_review-mutation-log.md`.\\n45-\\n46-Deferring the §7.3 Provider Manifest claim is the right call: nothing in this\\n47-delta implements the closed manifest, and re-binding it to a friendlier\\n48-symbol is the failure this board watches for. The stated reason for leaving\\n49-the ownership registry alone is accurate — `traceability.go:422` pins its\\n50-canonical projection to `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`.\\n51-\\n52-## Why this is not accepted\\n53-\\n--\\n199:## Logbook entry to land with the rework\\n200-\\n201:Not written to `LOGBOOK.md` by this run: the reviewer is read-only, and the\\n202-leaf must stay one commit past the checkpoint with a clean worktree. The\\n203-rework producer should land this verbatim above the 2026-09-04 0115 entry.\\n204-\\n205-```markdown\\n206-### 0210 — The inventory gate proves a site was reached, not that a test can tell which site fired\\n207-\\n208-- SCOPE: `internal/provider` review of TASK-260830-2890sd at `292fc6d`; §7.1. Five mutants survived the full suite.\\n209-- ROOT CAUSE: every `Verify` branch reports the same `integrity_failure` code, and every subtest asserts on `Code()` alone. `TestVerifyDetectsSubstitution/replaced_with_directory` (`trust_test.go:136`) sets `fakeFile{content: nil, ..., regular: false}` — two facts at once — so deleting the non-regular check at `provider.go:455-457` leaves the whole suite green. Isolated: with the fixture changed to `content: []byte(\\\"bytes\\\")` the subtest PASSes against intact production and FAILs with the check deleted.\\n210-- FINDING: this is entry 0115's own defect class, reintroduced one subtest away in the same function within the same commit that recorded fixing it. The refusal-inventory gate cannot see it. The gate requires each constructor site to be REACHED, and this fixture does reach `provider.go:456`; it cannot tell that a different site would have produced the same observable. By construction it also cannot see a DELETED site. Within one code class, \\\"site reached\\\" is not \\\"branch distinguished\\\" — the gate needs the test to assert which fact changed, and no test in the package asserts `Detail()`.\\n211-- FINDING: the trust gate is proven at 1 of 3 discovery sources. Injecting `if source == \\\"path\\\" { info.IsRegular = true; info.UID = owner.OperatorUID }` into `trustCandidate` survives, as does the same for every `source != \\\"plugin_dirs[0]\\\"`. All four refusal tests use a single `/plugins` directory; PATH is exercised only positively and for duplicates. Entry 1632's rule — enumerate every guard on the path and measure each over its own domain — applies per SOURCE here, not only per guard.\\n212-- FINDING: `Discover` refuses a relative `providers.plugin_dirs` entry (`provider.go:334`) but never checks `system.PathDirs()` (`provider.go:348`), and `OSSystem.Canonicalize` resolves relative entries against the process working directory via `filepath.Abs`. Probed through production `OSSystem`: `PATH=\\\".\\\"` admits an operator-owned `ax-provider-evil` planted in the CWD; `plugin_dirs=[\\\".\\\"]` refuses it. Two sources sharing `collectDirectory` do not share the gate. The owner check does not help — files in a checkout the operator owns are operator-owned.\\n213-- FINDING: the digest guard is proven at 1 of 32 bytes; narrowing `provider.go:466` to `sum[:1]` survives, because both fixture pairs happen to differ in their first byte. The `identity != record.owner` half of `provider.go:458` has no test at all — `adminUID = 7` is declared at `fake_test.go:118` and never used, and it is exactly the fixture that case needs.\\n214-- NOTE: §7.1 has no ownership binding at all — the registry binds 7.2/7.3/7.4/7.5/7.A/7.8/7.9 to the placeholder `catalog.ForRelease` and never mentions 7.1. The deferral reason is accurate (`traceability.go:422` pins the projection to `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`), but the section is now implemented and must be claimed before the Story closes.\\n215-- STATUS: Pending. TASK-260830-2890sd routed to `to-dev`; 27 of 32 mutants caught, production behaviour correct as written.\\n216-```\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 6761,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 1691,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}"}}}
