{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c695","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06e98-2a6f-7230-81f2-5355ec816701"},"sequence":421,"recorded_at":1780531400000837,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"e5781de4-97b1-4e5e-9466-20c1d6e7652b","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"e5781de4-97b1-4e5e-9466-20c1d6e7652b","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"e5781de4-97b1-4e5e-9466-20c1d6e7652b"},"call_id":"call_01a06e9910b471f2b0675ba129aa321c","text":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-15-1\",\n  \"command\": \"R=/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd; grep -n -i -B2 -A60 'Logbook entry' \\\"$R/TASK-260830-2890sd_review-verdict-rev2.md\\\" | head -120\",\n  \"description\": \"Find rev2 logbook entry\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"166-round inherits it.\\n167-\\n168:### F8 — the round-1 logbook entry was never landed\\n169-\\n170-The rev1 verdict supplied a complete entry with the instruction *\\\"The rework\\n171-producer should land this verbatim above the 2026-09-04 0115 entry.\\\"*\\n172-`LOGBOOK.md` is untouched in `292fc6d..d8fc669`, and its newest entry is still\\n173-`0115`. The round-2 findings — \\\"site reached is not branch distinguished\\\", the\\n174-1-of-3-sources gate, the 1-of-32-bytes digest, the relative-PATH bypass — exist\\n175-only in board resources.\\n176-\\n177:This matters beyond bookkeeping. LOGBOOK entry `1632` already records the\\n178-generalisation this round then repeated: *\\\"when a class of 'proven at one\\n179-sample' is found, the remedy is to enumerate every guard on that path and\\n180-measure each over its own domain, not to fix the one that was named.\\\"* The\\n181-round-2 rework fixed the four guards it was handed and did not sweep\\n182-`OwnerPolicy.Approves` — the very next guard on the same `trustCandidate` path\\n183-— or the `plugin_dirs` twin of the guard it was fixing. That is the third\\n184-recorded instance of the same failure, and the mechanism that was supposed to\\n185-propagate the lesson is the entry that did not get written.\\n186-\\n187-### F9 — the 3x4 table's source domain is a literal, and a fourth ungated source survives\\n188-\\n189-The orchestrator's round-2 brief asked directly whether the table's domains are\\n190-**derived** from the production discovery order rather than pinned as a\\n191-constant, and whether adding a fourth source without covering it reddens. The\\n192-answer is no, and no.\\n193-\\n194-`trustGateSources()` (`trust_sources_test.go:69-95`) returns a hand-written\\n195-three-element slice; `trustGateDimensions()` returns a hand-written four-element\\n196-slice. The reported ratio is\\n197-\\n198-```go\\n199-total := len(sources) * len(dimensions)   // 12\\n200-...\\n201-if passed != total { t.Fatalf(\\\"trust-gate coverage = %d/%d\\\", passed, total) }\\n202-```\\n203-\\n204-which compares the literal against itself. `12/12` is not a measurement of the\\n205-production source set; it is the same constant on both sides. `passed++` also\\n206-only runs when a subtest reaches the end of its closure, so a failing subtest\\n207-has already reddened before the ratio is consulted — the check adds nothing the\\n208-subtests do not already report.\\n209-\\n210-Demonstrated, not argued. Adding a fourth discovery source to `Discover` with\\n211-every trust gate bypassed:\\n212-\\n213-```go\\n214-\\tif names, err := system.ReadDir(\\\"/opt/ax-providers\\\"); err == nil {\\n215-\\t\\tfor _, name := range names {\\n216-\\t\\t\\tif id, ok, _ := externalID(name); ok {\\n217-\\t\\t\\t\\tif err := add(Candidate{id: id, kind: KindExternal, source: \\\"system\\\"}); err != nil {\\n218-\\t\\t\\t\\t\\treturn nil, err\\n219-\\t\\t\\t\\t}\\n220-\\t\\t\\t}\\n221-\\t\\t}\\n222-\\t}\\n223-```\\n224-\\n225-leaves `go test ./... -count=1` **green across all 14 packages** and `go vet`\\n226-clean. No shape check, no owner check, no digest, no canonicalisation — a whole\\n227-ungated source, invisible to a table that enumerates sources from a literal.\\n228-(The mutant reads a directory that does not exist on this host, so it is inert\\n229-in the fake and inert on the real filesystem; that inertness is the point. The\\n230-suite cannot distinguish \\\"source covered\\\" from \\\"source not enumerated\\\".)\\n231-\\n232-This package already owns the right pattern one file away.\\n233-`refusal_inventory_test.go` parses production source with `go/ast` and requires\\n234-a bijection between refusal constructor call sites and exercised negative\\n235-paths — and it is real: injecting a new unexercised refusal into `Verify` made\\n236-it fail naming `provider.go:451`. The trust-gate table should be derived the\\n237-same way: enumerate the `collectDirectory(...)` call sites and their source\\n--\\n334-`to-dev`. No production behaviour change is required. F6, F7 and F10 are test\\n335-work whose fixtures are written out above; F9 is one derived inventory built on\\n336:the pattern already in `refusal_inventory_test.go`; F8 is one LOGBOOK entry plus\\n337-the outcome refresh. Nothing found here is a stop-the-line boundary, and the\\n338-round-2 work itself is good: five findings closed, all five confirmed by\\n339-re-injecting their own mutants, and 34 of 38 mutants caught at this head.\\n340-\\n341-Two of the four survivors are the orchestrator's own round-2 questions answered\\n342-in the negative (F9, F10) — the fixes landed against the mutants that were\\n343-quoted rather than against the classes they belong to. That is the distinction\\n344-the brief asked to be checked, so it is the finding, not a technicality.\\n345-\\n346:## Logbook entry to land with the rework\\n347-\\n348-Not written by this run — the reviewer is read-only and the leaf must stay one\\n349-commit past the checkpoint with a clean worktree. Land this **and** the rev1\\n350-entry that was skipped, both above the `0115` entry, newest first.\\n351-\\n352-```markdown\\n353-### 0230 — The guard the round did not sweep was the one that matters: uid 0 admitted, suite green\\n354-\\n355-- SCOPE: `internal/provider` round-2 review of TASK-260830-2890sd at `d8fc669`; §7.1. 32 mutants, 30 caught, 2 survived.\\n356-- FINDING: injecting `if uid == 0 { return true }` into `OwnerPolicy.Approves` (`provider.go:143`) leaves `go test ./... -count=1` GREEN across all 14 packages. No test in the package uses uid 0 — `fakeUID = 1000`, `foreignUID = 2000`, `adminUID = 7`. The property is asserted three times and measured zero times: the production doc comment at `provider.go:139` (\\\"no superuser exception is implied\\\"), the outcome artifact (\\\"no implied superuser\\\"), and the §7.1 MUST pinned verbatim at `enumeration_test.go:135` (\\\"owned by the operator or an administrator-approved identity\\\"). Root-owned system PATH directories are the first case a real host meets, so this is the realistic identity, not an exotic one. Confirmed by isolation: a probe driving `Discover` with a uid-0 file under an operator-only policy PASSes against intact production and FAILs under the injection.\\n357-- ROOT CAUSE: entry 1632's generalisation, for the third recorded time. Round 2 closed the four guards the review named and did not enumerate the rest of the path. `OwnerPolicy.Approves` is the next guard inside `trustCandidate` after the two that were fixed, and `TestDiscoverRefusesRelativePluginDir` is the single-entry twin of the multi-entry PATH fixture the same round wrote — narrowing the `plugin_dirs` absolute gate to `index == 0` also survives the full suite. The remedy for \\\"proven at one sample\\\" is the traversal, not the named fix.\\n358:- NOTE: the rev1 verdict supplied a complete logbook entry with an instruction to land it verbatim, and `LOGBOOK.md` is untouched in `292fc6d..d8fc669`. The lesson that would have prevented this round's omission was written into a board resource and never reached the file the next round reads. A verdict-supplied entry is part of the rework scope, not an appendix to it.\\n359-- FINDING: two round-2 fixes closed the quoted mutant and not its class. The new 3x4 trust-gate table enumerates its sources from a hand-written literal and reports `12/12` as `len(sources) * len(dimensions)` compared against itself, so adding a fourth discovery source to `Discover` with every trust gate bypassed leaves `go test ./...` GREEN across all 14 packages. The package already owns the right pattern one file away — `refusal_inventory_test.go` derives refusal sites from the AST and reddens naming `provider.go:451` when an unexercised site is injected — and the table should derive its source labels from the `collectDirectory` call sites the same way. Separately, `TestVerifyDetectsLateByteDigestChange` moves the LAST digest byte, so `sum[:1]`, `sum[:16]`, `sum[:31]` and `sum[8:24]` all redden while `sum[1:]` SURVIVES: the guard moved from proven-at-byte-0 to proven-at-byte-31, the same 1-of-32 shape mirrored. A 32-subtest sweep over byte index costs microseconds and reports the ratio the domain actually has.\\n360-- NOTE: the round-2 fixes are real and were each re-confirmed by re-injecting the round-1 mutant at `d8fc669` — the 12-cell source x dimension table, the PATH absolute gate on both the fake and production `OSSystem` seams, the shape-only `Verify` fixture with per-branch `Detail()` pins, the late-byte digest fixture (kills both `sum[:1]` and `sum[:31]`), and the approved-administrator owner-identity subtest. Production behaviour is correct as written; only the evidence is short.\\n361-- STATUS: Pending. TASK-260830-2890sd routed to `to-dev`; no production behaviour change required.\\n362-```\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 8573,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 2144,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"bash","outcome":"success"}}}
